# Explore Finite Causality in Conway’s Life

Conway’s rule is local: one update reads a cell and its eight neighbors. This interactive notebook turns that simple observation into a finite simulation certificate. Begin with the [background on cellular automata](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cellular_automaton), then experiment directly.

## 1. The local rule

{{interactive_demo:1}}

The B3/S23 laboratory exposes every local case. The sparse-update implementation below applies the same condition simultaneously to all candidates near live cells.

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the exact local definition</summary>

For a configuration $c:\mathbb{Z}^2	o\{0,1\}$, let $
u_c(p)$ count the eight live Moore neighbors of $p$. The next state is alive exactly when $
u_c(p)=3$, or when $c(p)=1$ and $
u_c(p)=2$. See the [Moore neighborhood](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moore_neighborhood) for geometric context.

</details>

## 2. Walk backward through causality

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The depth-$t$ dependency cone starts with the target and repeatedly takes closed neighborhoods. Generate it with the iterative algorithm, then inspect its first few sizes.

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the finite-dependency proof sketch</summary>

At time zero, agreement at the target is enough. For the induction step, every cell read by the last update has a depth-$t$ cone contained in the target’s depth-$(t+1)$ cone. The induction hypothesis makes the two evolved configurations agree on the entire final closed neighborhood, and locality makes the target outputs equal.

</details>

## 3. Compare the guaranteed bound with overlap

Each closed neighborhood has nine cells, so a direct branching argument gives $|D_t(p)|\le 9^t$. On the square lattice, neighborhoods overlap and the observed cone is the $(2t+1)$-by-$(2t+1)$ square.

{{visualization:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the cardinality argument</summary>

The union bound gives $|D_{t+1}(p)|\le\sum_{q\in D_t(p)}|\overline N(q)|=9|D_t(p)|$. Starting from $|D_0(p)|=1$ yields $|D_t(p)|\le9^t$ by induction. The sharper square count is a future geometric refinement, not needed for this certificate.

</details>

## 4. Test the causal boundary

{{interactive_demo:2}}

Changes farther than $t$ in Chebyshev distance cannot reach the target in $t$ generations. The numerical independence demonstration places extra activity far outside the relevant cone.

{{demo:1}}

The memoized evaluator computes only spacetime states needed by one target:

{{algorithm:1}}

## 5. Patterns remain local

The familiar blinker illustrates synchronous evolution; it is an example, while the finite-causality theorem applies to every configuration, including infinite ones.

{{visualization:2}}

For a broader historical and conceptual introduction, visit the [Game of Life overview](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conway%27s_Game_of_Life).

<details><summary>What this does—and does not—establish</summary>

The established results give exact local semantics, empty-state stability, finite-speed dependence, and a $9^t$ simulation bound. A constructive universality theorem additionally needs proved signal carriers, gates, wires, crossings, memory, a clock, and an explicit compiler with noninterference and overhead bounds.

</details>

